# `Optional<T>` — no more nulls

A **box that visibly may or may not hold a `T`** — the type forces you to handle "absent."

## The problem it replaces

```java
User u = findUser(id);        // says User; might be null
System.out.println(u.name()); // NPE — far from the cause
```

`Optional<User>` puts "maybe" in the **type** — it can't be ignored.

## Create

```java
Optional.of(value)        // present; throws on null
Optional.ofNullable(ref)  // null → empty  (the bridge)
Optional.empty()
```

Avoid `if (isPresent()) get()` — the null-check in a new coat.

## Stay inside the box, then supply a fallback

```java
String name = findUser(id)
    .map(User::name)            // transform if present
    .filter(n -> !n.isBlank())
    .orElse("anonymous");       // leave with a default
```

`orElseGet(supplier)` lazy · `orElseThrow()` demand · `ifPresent(...)` act.

**Discipline:** a **return type** for "maybe no result." Not for fields, params, or in collections. Never `.get()` unchecked; never return `null` as an `Optional`.
